and now that distance learning is a  reality the community is getting  creative with ways to make it work  8 news now reporter kate houston shows  us how a dance studio in the northwest  is offering some relief for families  soon this studio and dance connection  will be  filled with desks like this one as they  help families navigate distance learning  for nearly two decades dance connection  has focused on supporting  and instructing young dancers now the  studio is preparing for a new routine  with their enrichment program d.c  cares it's important to me to keep  some kind of normalcy for these kids  owner sherrilyn bienin came up with the  concept this week  students will bring their own devices to  attend virtual classes during the day  they'll have their own desk where they  can focus on school work  and take part in distant social  interactions bienin tells me this can  help parents balance their schedules  whether it's a working parent or just a  parent who  doesn't want to keep fighting with their  child to get their work done  in addition to school work bienan wants  to offer some comfort through stress  management techniques  like yoga and stretching on there to  help them with their stress levels and  their anxiety through the whole process  as well as artistic outlets such as  dance journaling acting and  singing not only am i here to help  facilitate and navigate through the  remote learning  but i also want them to be able to still  be able to express themselves  and to have that performing arts as  their outlet  dc cares is available for grades k  through 8.  the 30 spots will be split up into  different rooms based on grade level  keith houston 8 news now d.c cares is an  enrichment program not a licensed  daycare they will not be giving  instruction but rather helping kids with  a pre-assigned  curriculum
 

